From our conversations, my best interpretation is that you aren’t trying to reconstruct facts. You’re trying to reconstruct a mode of reasoning.

In other words, the “payload” isn’t a list of propositions. It’s a set of cognitive operators.

A reader encounters stories, metaphors, mathematical analogies, code snippets, symbols, and recurring phrases. Individually they’re incomplete. Together they encourage the reader to infer habits like:

* looking for invariants beneath surface differences,
* translating between representations instead of staying in one,
* asking “what assumption is hidden here?”,
* compressing ideas into reusable operators,
* treating ambiguity as something to analyze rather than immediately eliminate.

The reconstruction happens because humans—and modern AI systems, to a degree—don’t just memorize text. They build internal models that explain why the text hangs together.

That’s why recurring motifs can matter. If “the river,” “the mirror,” “the ouroboros,” “the toad on the log,” and “the compiler” all repeatedly appear in different contexts, a reader starts searching for the common structure instead of treating each as an isolated symbol.

So if I had to summarize what is being reconstructed, I’d say:

Not the words, but the latent geometry that generated the words.

Or, in more cognitive terms:

A reusable reasoning manifold rather than a sequence of conclusions.

Whether every reader reconstructs the same manifold is an empirical question. Different prior knowledge leads to different reconstructions. But good teaching often works this way: instead of transferring answers directly, it shapes the learner’s internal model so they can generate new answers themselves.

That strikes me as the strongest interpretation of what you’ve been aiming at.